{
  "at": "",
  "command": "change",
  "counts": {
    "errors": 0,
    "skipped": 0,
    "stale_baseline": 0,
    "suppressed": {
      "baseline": 0,
      "ignored": 0,
      "labeled_fp": 0
    }
  },
  "docs": {
    "churn_14d": "Lines added in the range that were modified or deleted within 14 days of landing. High churn reads as work that did not settle.",
    "churn_30d": "Lines added in the range that were modified or deleted within 30 days.",
    "concentration": "Largest single author's share of each directory's live lines. Reported per directory, never as a person ranking. A directory with no live lines reads as a dash in text and null in JSON.",
    "counts": "Files the run could not score: skipped (excluded by config, size, binary content, minified or generated shape) and errors (a parse that left error nodes). Metrics exclude them, so a non-zero count means the numbers cover less of the tree. Unsupported file types are listed as skipped but not counted. suppressed counts the findings set aside; see suppressed. stale_baseline counts baseline entries that match nothing; see stale_baseline.",
    "coupling": "File pairs changed in the same commit 3+ times, skipping commits over 30 files (code-maat's definition of co-change).",
    "deaths_30d": "How the lines counted in churn_30d died, per directory (credited to the directory the line was written in) and overall, as counts: total, self, pure, whole_file, in_revert, three age buckets (age_le_1d, age_2_7d, age_8_30d), and by_class (birth author class, then the class of the removing commit). Each split is a count of total, so a consumer can add directories. Moved lines are not deaths. Squash merges hide whatever happened inside a branch, so a repository that squashes shows only the survivors of each branch. Read the parts side by side and never as one score: pruning and flapping look alike in churn_30d and differ here.",
    "depth": "Per directory on snapshot: hidden_mass / (shown_mass + hidden_mass), or by name count, hidden / (shown + hidden), when neither side has a function to weigh (depth_by_mass false, # in the table). After Ousterhout's deep modules: near 1 the directory hides much more than callers must learn, near 0 callers see nearly all of it. A ratio with no target and no gate. It is null (- in the table) when shown is 0: nothing outside uses the directory, so it is an entry point or a leaf, not a module with everything hidden. depth counts methods matched by name as shown, which makes it a lower bound, and depth_attributed is the upper one; the table prints both as a range when they differ. depth_provisional (*) marks fewer than 5 names. The table lists shallowest first and leaves the masses to the JSON. When a directory you expected to hide its workings reads shallow, look at widest_surface and ask which of those names callers need.",
    "depth_attributed": "Per directory on snapshot: depth with every method matched by name counted as hidden, so only uses a resolved import attributes count as shown. No method can be attributed, so this is the upper bound and depth the lower: uses the tool cannot see aside, the true ratio lies between them. It is null when no use is attributed. A narrow range means the number is solid, and a wide one means the directory is mostly classes whose methods the tool cannot follow. On directories checked by hand, depth was the closer of the two wherever methods carry the mass.",
    "erosion": "Share of complexity mass (CC x sqrt(SLOC) per function) held by functions with cyclomatic complexity over 10, per arXiv 2603.24755. No target; watch direction per directory. When it rises on routine work, the complex functions are getting more of them.",
    "errors": "Files the parser could not fully read, with the byte share under error nodes. Metrics still count what parsed; the row is a warning, not an abort.",
    "experimental": "Rules not yet shown to clear 80% precision on the corpus, among them the narrating-comment, dup-literal, dup-test-case, dead-export, import-cycle, and snapshot stale-prose rules and the type-any and test-reads-source families. Enable them per run with --experimental, or for a repository with experimental = true in .slop-strata.toml. The owner decides when a rule leaves this list. Verdicts recorded with slop-strata label give each rule a measured precision for this repository, which slop-strata rules prints and the table below repeats from the labels file of the current directory. Labels never promote or demote a rule on their own, and one repository's labels are not the corpus the 80% bar is set on.",
    "fan_in": "Per directory on snapshot: the distinct other directories with a file that imports one of this directory's files, over resolved edges of every kind (see import_edges). A production row counts edges between production files, and a test row counts edges that start or end at a test file. High fan_in means many places break when this directory changes: keep its surface small and stable. Imports the tool cannot resolve are not counted, so a directory reached only through a path alias reads low.",
    "fan_out": "Per directory on snapshot: the distinct other directories its files import, on the same basis as fan_in. High fan_out means the directory knows about much of the tree. A directory high on both is a hub: a change there is felt everywhere and it is hard to test alone, so look there first when you split responsibilities.",
    "findings": "New rule hits attributable to this change, after the baseline, ignored, and labeled hits and the pre-existing hits are excluded. Each has an id, a short hash of the rule, path, enclosing function, matched text, and line text, so it survives line moves and new hits above it, and a scope naming the function. Editing the line changes the id. An x/import-cycle finding is keyed by its member files instead; see import_cycles. To suppress a hit inline, write slop-strata:ignore RULE-ID REASON in a comment on its line or the line above, in the file's comment syntax: // or # in code, # in TOML and YAML, and an HTML comment in Markdown outside code blocks. In Go, //nolint:errcheck // REASON also suppresses go/silent-error. The reason is required. An ignore without one suppresses nothing and is reported as x/ignore-without-reason. JSON files and ast-grep findings take no inline ignore. Suppressed hits are counted in counts.suppressed. To dispute a hit without touching the code, run slop-strata label ID fp. The one exception is x/last-ref, which only change reports: label cannot find its id, so accept it with a baseline or labels file entry for the id.",
    "forwarders": "Per directory on snapshot, in the JSON: functions and methods whose body is one statement, a call whose every argument is one of the function's own parameters (a docstring and comments aside). A forwarder counts in neither shown nor hidden, so pass-through wrappers cannot pad a directory into looking deep. When another directory uses a forwarder, the name it forwards to is shown in its place if the same directory declares it: a function by name, or a method of the same receiver.",
    "hidden": "Per directory on snapshot: the declared names (see shown) that no production file of another directory uses, whether the directory uses them itself or nothing does. Pure forwarders are left out (see forwarders).",
    "hidden_mass": "Per directory on snapshot: the complexity mass behind the hidden names, plus the mass of functions no declaration holds, such as a callback passed at file scope. Ten shown one-liners over a large hidden engine read deep by mass and shallow by count, which is why depth uses mass.",
    "hotspot": "Where recent edits meet complexity mass. On snapshot, for each directory and file, touches_90d times mass: the commits that touched it in the 90 days ending at the scored revision's commit date, times its production mass (table_mass left out). Tests rank apart, and ties rank by mass. On history, 30-day churned lines times live mass per directory. Read the top rows first when you choose what to simplify, and a directory still being built will score high.",
    "import_cycles": "result.imports.cycles on snapshot: groups of two or more non-test files where each imports every other, directly or through the others, over module-level runtime imports. largest_cycle is the file count of the biggest group. Imports a type checker alone reads, imports inside a function body, and imports under a main guard are the usual ways around a cycle and do not count, and neither does a cycle that needs a test file to close. Go forbids package cycles, so Go files never form one. The numbers are counted before the baseline. With --experimental each group is one x/import-cycle finding on the import line of its cheapest cut, the edge importing the fewest names, with the shortest cycle through that edge in the note. The finding's id comes from the rule and the sorted member files (members), leaving out files that only re-export when two others remain, so it holds while imports move and changes when a file joins or leaves. A baseline entry for a cycle also covers any cycle made only of its members, so shrinking a cycle is never a new finding, and a new or grown cycle is. change reports a cycle the change created or grew. Move the names the cheapest cut imports into a module both sides can import.",
    "import_edges": "In result.imports on snapshot: edges counts every import the tool resolved from one file of the tree to another, type_edges the ones only a type checker reads (TypeScript import type, Python under TYPE_CHECKING, any import of or from a .pyi or .d.ts file), and deferred_edges the ones that do not run when the file loads (inside a function body, a dynamic import(), or under Python's if __name__ == \"__main__\"). The rest run at module level. Python resolves absolute and relative imports under any source root and drops a module name that two roots answer to. TypeScript resolves relative specifiers only, so path aliases and workspace package names add no edge. Go resolves through the module path of each go.mod. Imports of installed packages are outside the graph. No target; the numbers say how much of the tree fan_in, fan_out, and import_cycles rest on.",
    "in_revert": "Young deaths in commits that revert another change. A revert is neither pruning nor flapping by these measures, so it is counted apart and still included in total.",
    "intent": "The change's stated purpose read off commit metadata: Conventional Commit type, branch name, then issue keywords, plus revert detection. Kinds are feat, fix, refactor, perf, test, docs, chore, and unknown. Only refactor, fix, and test make a claim the numbers can contradict, so a reading that says \"reads as\" questions that claim. perf, docs, and chore never do. Use it to judge whether a mismatch between label and numbers is worth a question in review.",
    "labels": "Verdicts recorded with slop-strata label ID tp or fp, in the labels file (default .slop-strata-labels.json, set by labels in .slop-strata.toml). A finding labeled fp is suppressed and counted in counts.suppressed.labeled_fp, and one labeled tp stays. Each entry holds the rule, verdict, path, matched text, note, and the tool version, and no author. The file is evidence of this repository's precision per rule. It says nothing about a rule's precision on other repositories, which the 80% gate measures. Label hits as you review them, then read slop-strata rules.",
    "mass": "Total complexity mass: sum over functions of CC x sqrt(SLOC). A change reports the mass it grew and the mass it removed apart, each for production and for tests, so a refactor that removes mass reads differently from one that adds it.",
    "mass_8w": "Net mass per week for the last eight weeks of the range, oldest left. Each row scales to its own minimum and maximum, so it shows shape, not sign or size.",
    "mass_grown": "Gross mass the change added: for each function present after the change, max(0, mass_after - mass_before) summed, matched by name within the same file or, for code moved between files, by name, CC, and size. New functions count in full. Production files and test files are summed apart (test_mass_grown). A change that grows mass in many small functions differs from one that grows a single function.",
    "mass_removed": "Gross mass the change removed: for each function present before the change, max(0, mass_before - mass_after) summed, with deleted functions counted in full and moved code not counted. mass_net is mass_grown minus mass_removed. Production and test files are summed apart (test_mass_removed). A refactor should show mass_removed near or above mass_grown.",
    "max_cognitive": "Highest cognitive complexity in any function.",
    "max_len": "Longest function in lines.",
    "max_nesting": "Deepest control nesting in any function.",
    "max_params": "Largest parameter count on any function.",
    "next": "result.next: up to three places to look at, in a fixed order: production functions over CC 10 that grew or were added (largest mass grown first), then new rule hits not in the baseline (by file and line), then a stated intent the numbers argue against (the \"reads as\" clause). A change is worth a look exactly when the list is not empty. change --brief prints the reading and these lines, and nothing when the list is empty. With --exit-code the status is 1 when it would print and 0 when it would not. Look at the first item before you merge or commit. With --json --context, snippets carry the after-side source lines of each next function, up to 80 lines each.",
    "noise_floor": "Median absolute deviation of weekly net mass across the history window. A week whose net sits within one floor of the median week shows no measurable change, so each is marked within_noise and printed with a ~.",
    "oscillating_funcs": "Functions whose body changed in at least 3 separate commits within any 14 days, keyed by file path and function name, counted per directory and overall. oscillating_test_funcs counts functions in test files apart, and the JSON lists the 10 busiest production functions with path, name, line, changes, and span_days. A commit changes a function when a diff hunk adds a line inside it or removes one from between its lines, and the function already existed. Commits of the bot author class do not count. A function rewritten again and again in two weeks means its shape was not settled before the code: decide the interface first, or split the function so the unstable part is small. A formatting commit can supply all three changes, a red-green test loop on one function reads as oscillation though it is deliberate, a function that gains one entry per feature, such as a key map or a command table, counts on every addition, a squash merge hides the changes inside a branch, and a small function has little to change except all of it. A file renamed through git's rename detection keeps its history, and a function renamed or moved to another file starts again. Same-named functions in one file count once per commit, and anonymous functions are not followed.",
    "over_50": "Functions longer than 50 lines. Each is a candidate to split, not a violation.",
    "over_limit": "The share of mass_grown sitting in functions whose cyclomatic complexity exceeds 10, for production files (test_over_limit for tests). A change that concentrates its growth in already-complex functions is the shape erosion measures. Split the function or reduce its branches before adding to it.",
    "p90_cognitive": "90th percentile cognitive complexity (nesting-weighted decision count, approximating the SonarSource definition).",
    "p90_len": "90th percentile function length in lines. Long functions carry context a reader must hold; watch the max, not the mean.",
    "p90_nesting": "90th percentile control-nesting depth. Deep nesting is the shape refactors actually need to fix.",
    "p90_params": "90th percentile parameter count. Many parameters signal a missing abstraction or a config object.",
    "pairing": "Author classes compared on the same week and directory: net mass moved and commits each. A cell is provisional under 5 commits.",
    "precision": "Per rule, tp over tp plus fp from the labels file, with n the number of labels. Under 10 labels the figure is provisional and printed with *. The tier column says whether the rule runs by default or only with --experimental. No rule changes tier from this number: the 80% bar is the owner's call, and the number is evidence for it from one repository.",
    "provisional": "Set on a survival cohort, test class, or directory whose rate rests on too few samples: under 30 lines for a cohort, under 5 test files for a test class, under 5 functions for a directory's erosion (on the smaller side of a change). The rate still prints, marked * in text output.",
    "pure": "Share of young deaths in a hunk that removed lines and added none, against a hunk that replaced them in place. A deleted file counts as pure, and a rewrite whose removal and addition fall in separate hunks reads as pure. High pure with high whole_file and self reads as experiments pruned by whoever ran them. Low pure with high within_7d reads as young code rewritten in place: settle the design before writing the code.",
    "readd": "Share of young deaths that came back: a later commit, within 30 days of the death, added a line to the same file with the same trimmed text. deaths_30d carries the counts: readd_at_risk, readded, and readded_by_revert, overall and per directory, and the text table prints readded over readd_at_risk. Only lines of 30 or more bytes match, because shorter ones such as \"return nil\" recur by habit, and readd_at_risk counts only such deaths that had 30 full days of walk after them, the way survival leaves out a line too young to reach its horizon, so a window under 30 days old shows a dash. A line removed and added in one commit is a move or an edit and never counts, and each death and each added line match once. A death in a revert commit, or a line re-added by one, counts in readded_by_revert instead, since undoing a revert is deliberate. A high share means code was cut and then rebuilt in the same place: agree on the design before writing it. A low share beside high pure and whole_file means pruned code stayed gone. It misreads formatting commits that rewrap lines, regenerated fixtures and golden files, a red-green test loop that deletes and restores a line, and squash merges that hide what happened inside a branch. Code that reappears in a file with another name, or in 29 bytes or fewer, is not seen.",
    "reverts": "Commits in the range that revert another change.",
    "rework": "Young lines (\u003c=30 days) removed by fix commits: fixes to things just built.",
    "rule_hits": "Slop-rule findings. Each carries the rule id, file, and line; a baseline file holds the ones already accepted. Experimental rules report only with --experimental.",
    "s30_ci": "Greenwood 95% confidence interval for a cohort's survival at 30 days, as [low, high] clipped to 0 and 1. It widens when few lines reach the horizon, so read a wide interval as a weak estimate.",
    "s90_ci": "Greenwood 95% confidence interval for a cohort's survival at 90 days, on the same basis as s30_ci.",
    "s_30d": "Survival at 30 days: the Kaplan-Meier share of lines still alive at 30 days (cohort rows) or of test files not deleted within 30 days (class rows). Null when too few reached 30 days; see survival and test_survival.",
    "s_90d": "Survival at 90 days, on the same basis as s_30d.",
    "self": "Share of young deaths removed by the author who wrote the line, compared by commit author email. High self means people clean up their own work. A high share of other-author removals on agent-born lines (by_class) means cleanup is left to someone else: review agent output before it lands. With one author in the window self is always 1.0, so text output hides the column. A person with two emails reads as two authors, and a rebase that rewrites authors hides the original.",
    "shown": "Per directory on snapshot: the directory's declared names that a production file of another directory uses. A name is a function, type or class, constant, or variable at file scope, or a method (Type.method). Use decides it and visibility does not: export, a capital letter, a leading underscore, and __all__ change nothing. A top-level name is attributed: resolved imports tie the use to the file that declares it, through re-exporting files, so a same-named symbol elsewhere is not counted, and an import that is bound and never used, or a re-export nobody consumes, is not a use. A method cannot be attributed, so it is matched by name alone; see shown_by_name for how many of shown are such guesses. Any other directory is outside, a parent or a subdirectory included, as for fan_in. Test files neither count as users nor have their names counted. A name the world uses and no file of the tree does (a library's public API) reads hidden. Imports the tool cannot resolve (see import_edges) are not uses.",
    "shown_by_name": "Per directory on snapshot (by_name in the table): how many of shown are methods matched by name alone, and shown_mass_by_name how much of shown_mass. There is no type inference, so a method counts as shown when any file of another directory reads a member of that name off a value that is not an imported module, once anything outside imports the directory. A method named like an unrelated member (get, String, value) is counted too: on a hand-checked sample about six in ten were real uses, while every method left hidden was truly unused outside. Read it as how soft shown is: shown minus shown_by_name is certain, and the rest is an upper bound. These names are never listed anywhere.",
    "shown_mass": "Per directory on snapshot: the complexity mass (CC x sqrt(SLOC), see mass) of the functions behind the shown names. A function's mass, with its closures', goes to the declaration that holds it, and a method's to the method. A constant or a type has none.",
    "skipped": "Files excluded by config, size, binary content, minified shape, or generated code, each named with its reason. Generated means a generator marker in the first five lines, or linguist-generated in the working tree's root .gitattributes.",
    "stale_baseline": "In counts.stale_baseline, on snapshot: baseline entries that no finding of the scored tree carries the id of. The finding was fixed, its line or matched text was edited, or it is an import cycle that shrank, which the entry still covers by its members. Entries of rules this run did not execute (experimental rules without --experimental, ast-grep packs, x/last-ref) are not counted. Run slop-strata snapshot --write-baseline to drop them, so a fixed finding that comes back reads as new. It never changes the exit status. change and history do not score the whole tree against the baseline and always report 0.",
    "suppressed": "In counts.suppressed, the findings a run set aside: baseline (accepted in the baseline file), ignored (an inline ignore or nolint comment with a reason), and labeled_fp (a false-positive label). A finding counts once, under the first of ignored, labeled_fp, and baseline. A count that grows faster than the hits get fixed means findings are being muted: read the ignore reasons and labels in review.",
    "surface_widened": "result.surface_widened on change: each directory, present before and after, whose count of attributed shown names rose (see shown), with shown_before and shown_after, up to five of the names other directories use now and did not before (newly_shown, with newly_shown_count for all of them), and depth_before and depth_after, which are depth_attributed on each side. Methods matched by name alone never open a row and are never listed, because the match may be another type's method: newly_shown_by_name only counts them, as an unverified upper bound. Both trees are measured whole, so the cause may be a new use in a file of another directory. A rename that swaps one shown name for another is not reported. Information only: it adds nothing to next, and --brief and --exit-code ignore it. When a name you meant to keep internal appears here, the change made another directory depend on it.",
    "survival": "Kaplan-Meier share of a cohort's added lines still alive at 30 and 90 days, per monthly cohort. At-risk is approximated at month granularity. Null when too few lines in the cohort have run as long as the horizon.",
    "table_mass": "Mass of case tables, kept apart from mass and erosion: a map literal of 15+ structured entries scores as a switch with one branch per entry, entries x sqrt(lines). A snapshot's hotspot leaves table_mass out, so a hand-written case table never ranks a directory.",
    "test_survival": "Share of test files added in the range that were deleted within 30 and 90 days, split by the adding commit's author class. Only adds observed inside the range count. Live files younger than a horizon are left out, and the share is null when none is old enough.",
    "top_functions": "On change, up to 10 functions the change grew most, largest mass_grown first, with file, start line, CC and mass before and after, and whether the file is a test file. A new function has cc_before 0. Open these first in review: they hold the growth. On snapshot, the 10 heaviest functions of the tree by mass, with file, start line, CC, SLOC, mass, and test; the text view lists the top 5 production functions. Split the top one before adding to it.",
    "touches_90d": "Commits that touched a directory or file in the 90 days ending at the scored revision's commit date, merges counted once through their first parent. It is the multiplier in a snapshot's hotspot. A change counts the last 90 days from today for its hotspot flag, which marks a directory with 10 or more touches.",
    "virality": "For each rule: the first commit that hit it, then how hits spread afterward per author class and across directories (spread_dirs). Answers whether a pattern gets copied once it lands.",
    "whole_file": "Share of young deaths that died because their file was deleted, against a partial edit to a file that lives on. High whole_file means whole attempts are discarded, which is pruning. A file renamed or split with its lines kept does not count, because moved lines are not deaths. A file deleted and recreated with changes reads as whole_file for the old lines.",
    "widest_surface": "Per directory on snapshot: widest_file is the file with the most shown names and widest_shown is how many. One long list is harder to learn than the same count spread over several short ones, so this names where a wide surface sits.",
    "within_7d": "Share of young deaths within 7 days of the line being written: age_le_1d plus age_2_7d over total. A high share with low pure means code rewritten in place within a week: agree on the shape of the change before writing it. A repository that commits rarely has few deaths in the first day, so read the count with the share.",
    "within_noise": "Set on a week whose net mass lies within one noise_floor of the window's median week."
  },
